# Every text file checks out with LF on every OS.
#
# Atlas's tooling reads and writes LF: oxfmt, the theme-key generator
# (`scripts/generate-theme-keys.mjs`, whose drift check compares generated
# files byte-for-byte) and the source-scanning contract suites in `tests/`
# (whose regexes anchor on `\n`). Git for Windows defaults to
# `core.autocrlf=true`, which converts every text file to CRLF on checkout —
# on the first Windows clone that failed 16 contract tests before a single
# line had been changed, and with them the pre-commit hook. Declaring the
# line endings here makes the checkout the same on macOS, Linux and Windows,
# whatever the machine's `core.autocrlf` says.
#
# `text=auto` lets Git detect binary files by content and leave them alone.
# `vendor/atlas-engine/apply-patch/tests/fixtures/scenarios/.gitattributes` marks the
# engine's CRLF-preservation fixtures `-text`, and a nested file wins over
# this one for its subtree, so those bytes stay exactly as committed.
#
# An existing Windows clone picks this up on its next checkout; to refresh a
# worktree that was already converted, run `git rm --cached -r -q . && git
# reset --hard -q` on a clean tree.
* text=auto eol=lf
